  <title>Chairman's Letter - 1979</title>
</head>
<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>
<pre>
<i>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</i>

     Again, we must lead off with a few words about accounting. =20
Since our last annual report, the accounting profession has=20
decided that equity securities owned by insurance companies must=20
be carried on the balance sheet at market value.  We previously=20
have carried such equity securities at the lower of aggregate=20
cost or aggregate market value.  Because we have large unrealized=20
gains in our insurance equity holdings, the result of this new=20
policy is to increase substantially both the 1978 and 1979=20
yearend net worth, even after the appropriate liability is=20
established for taxes on capital gains that would be payable=20
should equities be sold at such market valuations.

     As you know, Blue Chip Stamps, our 60% owned subsidiary, is=20
fully consolidated in Berkshire Hathaway=92s financial statements. =20
However, Blue Chip still is required to carry its equity=20
investments at the lower of aggregate cost or aggregate market=20
value, just as Berkshire Hathaway=92s insurance subsidiaries did=20
prior to this year.  Should the same equities be purchased at an=20
identical price by an insurance subsidiary of Berkshire Hathaway=20
and by Blue Chip Stamps, present accounting principles often=20
would require that they end up carried on our consolidated=20
balance sheet at two different values. (That should keep you on=20
your toes.) Market values of Blue Chip Stamps=92 equity holdings=20
are given in footnote 3 on page 18.

<i>1979 Operating Results</i>

     We continue to feel that the ratio of operating earnings=20
(before securities gains or losses) to shareholders=92 equity <i>with=20
all securities valued at cost</i> is the most appropriate way to=20
measure any single year=92s operating performance.

     Measuring such results against shareholders=92 equity with=20
securities valued at market could significantly distort the=20
operating performance percentage because of wide year-to-year=20
market value changes in the net worth figure that serves as the=20
denominator.  For example, a large decline in securities values=20
could result in a very low =93market value=94 net worth that, in=20
turn, could cause mediocre operating earnings to look=20
unrealistically good.  Alternatively, the more successful that=20
equity investments have been, the larger the net worth base=20
becomes and the poorer the operating performance figure appears. =20
Therefore, we will continue to report operating performance=20
measured against beginning net worth, with securities valued at=20
cost.

     On this basis, we had a reasonably good operating=20
performance in 1979 - but not quite as good as that of 1978 -=20
with operating earnings amounting to 18.6% of beginning net=20
worth.  Earnings per share, of course, increased somewhat (about=20
20%) but we regard this as an improper figure upon which to=20
focus.  We had substantially more capital to work with in 1979=20
than in 1978, and our performance in utilizing that capital fell=20
short of the earlier year, even though per-share earnings rose. =20
=93Earnings per share=94 will rise constantly on a dormant savings=20
account or on a U.S. Savings Bond bearing a fixed rate of return=20
simply because =93earnings=94 (the stated interest rate) are=20
continuously plowed back and added to the capital base.  Thus,=20
even a =93stopped clock=94 can look like a growth stock if the=20
dividend payout ratio is low.

     The primary test of managerial economic performance is the=20
achievement of a high earnings rate on equity capital employed=20
(without undue leverage, accounting gimmickry, etc.) and not the=20
achievement of consistent gains in earnings per share.  In our=20
view, many businesses would be better understood by their=20
shareholder owners, as well as the general public, if managements=20
and financial analysts modified the primary emphasis they place=20
upon earnings per share, and upon yearly changes in that figure.

<i>Long Term Results</i>

     In measuring long term economic performance - in contrast to=20
yearly performance - we believe it is appropriate to recognize=20
fully any realized capital gains or losses as well as=20
extraordinary items, and also to utilize financial statements=20
presenting equity securities at market value.  Such capital gains=20
or losses, either realized or unrealized, are fully as important=20
to shareholders over a period of years as earnings realized in a=20
more routine manner through operations; it is just that their=20
impact is often extremely capricious in the short run, a=20
characteristic that makes them inappropriate as an indicator of=20
single year managerial performance.

     The book value per share of Berkshire Hathaway on September=20
30, 1964 (the fiscal yearend prior to the time that your present=20
management assumed responsibility) was $19.46 per share.  At=20
yearend 1979, book value with equity holdings carried at market=20
value was $335.85 per share.  The gain in book value comes to=20
20.5% compounded annually.  This figure, of course, is far higher=20
than any average of our yearly operating earnings calculations,=20
and reflects the importance of capital appreciation of insurance=20
equity investments in determining the overall results for our=20
shareholders.  It probably also is fair to say that the quoted=20
book value in 1964 somewhat overstated the intrinsic value of the=20
enterprise, since the assets owned at that time on either a going=20
concern basis or a liquidating value basis were not worth 100=20
cents on the dollar. (The liabilities were solid, however.)

     We have achieved this result while utilizing a low amount of=20
leverage (both financial leverage measured by debt to equity, and=20
operating leverage measured by premium volume to capital funds of=20
our insurance business), and also without significant issuance or=20
repurchase of shares.  Basically, we have worked with the capital=20
with which we started.  From our textile base we, or our Blue=20
Chip and Wesco subsidiaries, have acquired total ownership of=20
thirteen businesses through negotiated purchases from private=20
owners for cash, and have started six others. (It=92s worth a=20
mention that those who have sold to us have, almost without=20
exception, treated us with exceptional honor and fairness, both=20
at the time of sale and subsequently.)

     But before we drown in a sea of self-congratulation, a=20
further - and crucial - observation must be made.  A few years=20
ago, a business whose per-share net worth compounded at 20%=20
annually would have guaranteed its owners a highly successful=20
real investment return.  Now such an outcome seems less certain. =20
For the inflation rate, coupled with individual tax rates, will=20
be the ultimate determinant as to whether our internal operating=20
performance produces successful investment results - i.e., a=20
reasonable gain in purchasing power from funds committed - for=20
you as shareholders.

     Just as the original 3% savings bond, a 5% passbook savings=20
account or an 8% U.S. Treasury Note have, in turn, been=20
transformed by inflation into financial instruments that chew up,=20
rather than enhance, purchasing power over their investment=20
lives, a business earning 20% on capital can produce a negative=20
real return for its owners under inflationary conditions not much=20
more severe than presently prevail.

     If we should continue to achieve a 20% compounded gain - not=20
an easy or certain result by any means - and this gain is=20
translated into a corresponding increase in the market value of=20
Berkshire Hathaway stock as it has been over the last fifteen=20
years, your after-tax purchasing power gain is likely to be very=20
close to zero at a 14% inflation rate.  Most of the remaining six=20
percentage points will go for income tax any time you wish to=20
convert your twenty percentage points of nominal annual gain into=20
cash.

     That combination - the inflation rate plus the percentage of=20
capital that must be paid by the owner to transfer into his own=20
pocket the annual earnings achieved by the business (i.e.,=20
ordinary income tax on dividends and capital gains tax on=20
retained earnings) - can be thought of as an =93investor=92s misery=20
index=94.  When this index exceeds the rate of return earned on=20
equity by the business, the investor=92s purchasing power (real=20
capital) shrinks even though he consumes nothing at all.  <i>We have=20
no corporate solution to this problem; high inflation rates will=20
not help us earn higher rates of return on equity.</i>

     One friendly but sharp-eyed commentator on Berkshire has=20
pointed out that our book value at the end of 1964 would have=20
bought about one-half ounce of gold and, fifteen years later,=20
after we have plowed back all earnings along with much blood,=20
sweat and tears, the book value produced will buy about the same=20
half ounce.  A similar comparison could be drawn with Middle=20
Eastern oil.  The rub has been that government has been=20
exceptionally able in printing money and creating promises, but=20
is unable to print gold or create oil.

     We intend to continue to do as well as we can in managing=20
the internal affairs of the business.  But you should understand=20
that external conditions affecting the stability of currency may=20
very well be the most important factor in determining whether=20
there are any real rewards from your investment in Berkshire=20
Hathaway.

