     Blue Chip and Wesco are public companies with reporting=20
requirements of their own.  On pages 37-43 of this report, we=20
have reproduced the narrative reports of the principal executives=20
of both companies, in which they describe 1979 operations.  Some=20
of the numbers they mention in their reports are not precisely=20
identical to those in the above table because of accounting and=20
tax complexities. (The Yanomamo Indians employ only three=20
numbers: one, two, and more than two.  Maybe their time will=20
come.) However, the commentary in those reports should be helpful=20
to you in understanding the underlying economic characteristics=20
and future prospects of the important businesses that they=20
manage.

     A copy of the full annual report of either company will be=20
mailed to any shareholder of Berkshire upon request to Mr. =20
Robert H. Bird for Blue Chip Stamps, 5801 South Eastern Avenue,=20
Los Angeles, California 90040, or to Mrs. Bette Deckard for Wesco=20
Financial Corporation, 315 East Colorado Boulevard, Pasadena,=20
California 91109.

<i>Textiles and Retailing</i>

     The relative significance of these two areas has diminished=20
somewhat over the years as our insurance business has grown=20
dramatically in size and earnings.  Ben Rosner, at Associated=20
Retail Stores, continues to pull rabbits out of the hat - big=20
rabbits from a small hat.  Year after year, he produces very=20
large earnings relative to capital employed - realized in cash=20
and not in increased receivables and inventories as in many other=20
retail businesses - in a segment of the market with little growth=20
and unexciting demographics.  Ben is now 76 and, like our other=20
=93up-and-comers=94, Gene Abegg, 82, at Illinois National and Louis=20
Vincenti, 74, at Wesco, regularly achieves more each year.

     Our textile business also continues to produce some cash,=20
but at a low rate compared to capital employed.  This is not a=20
reflection on the managers, but rather on the industry in which=20
they operate.  In some businesses - a network TV station, for=20
example - it is virtually impossible to avoid earning=20
extraordinary returns on tangible capital employed in the=20
business.  And assets in such businesses sell at equally=20
extraordinary prices, one thousand cents or more on the dollar, a=20
valuation reflecting the splendid, almost unavoidable, economic=20
results obtainable.  Despite a fancy price tag, the =93easy=94=20
business may be the better route to go.

     We can speak from experience, having tried the other route. =20
Your Chairman made the decision a few years ago to purchase=20
Waumbec Mills in Manchester, New Hampshire, thereby expanding our=20
textile commitment.  By any statistical test, the purchase price=20
was an extraordinary bargain; we bought well below the working=20
capital of the business and, in effect, got very substantial=20
amounts of machinery and real estate for less than nothing.  But=20
the purchase was a mistake.  While we labored mightily, new=20
problems arose as fast as old problems were tamed.

     Both our operating and investment experience cause us to=20
conclude that =93turnarounds=94 seldom turn, and that the same=20
energies and talent are much better employed in a good business=20
purchased at a fair price than in a poor business purchased at a=20
bargain price.  Although a mistake, the Waumbec acquisition has=20
not been a disaster.  Certain portions of the operation are=20
proving to be valuable additions to our decorator line (our=20
strongest franchise) at New Bedford, and it=92s possible that we=20
may be able to run profitably on a considerably reduced scale at=20
Manchester.  However, our original rationale did not prove out.

<i>Insurance Underwriting</i>

     We predicted last year that the combined underwriting ratio=20
(see definition on page 36) for the insurance industry would=20
=93move up at least a few points, perhaps enough to throw the=20
industry as a whole into an underwriting loss position=94.  That is=20
just about the way it worked out.  The industry underwriting=20
ratio rose in 1979 over three points, from roughly 97.4% to=20
100.7%. We also said that we thought our underwriting performance=20
relative to the industry would improve somewhat in 1979 and,=20
again, things worked out as expected.  Our own underwriting ratio=20
actually decreased from 98.2% to 97.1%. Our forecast for 1980 is=20
similar in one respect; again we feel that the industry=92s=20
performance will worsen by at least another few points.  However,=20
this year we have no reason to think that our performance=20
relative to the industry will further improve. (Don=92t worry - we=20
won=92t hold back to try to validate that forecast.)

     Really extraordinary results were turned in by the portion=20
of National Indemnity Company=92s insurance operation run by Phil=20
Liesche.  Aided by Roland Miller in Underwriting and Bill Lyons=20
in Claims, this section of the business produced an underwriting=20
profit of $8.4 million on about $82 million of earned premiums. =20
Only a very few companies in the entire industry produced a=20
result comparable to this.

     You will notice that earned premiums in this segment were=20
down somewhat from those of 1978.  We hear a great many insurance=20
managers talk about being willing to reduce volume in order to=20
underwrite profitably, but we find that very few actually do so. =20
Phil Liesche is an exception: if business makes sense, he writes=20
it; if it doesn=92t, he rejects it.  It is our policy not to lay=20
off people because of the large fluctuations in work load=20
produced by such voluntary volume changes.  We would rather have=20
some slack in the organization from time to time than keep=20
everyone terribly busy writing business on which we are going to=20
lose money.  Jack Ringwalt, the founder of National Indemnity=20
Company, instilled this underwriting discipline at the inception=20
of the company, and Phil Liesche never has wavered in maintaining=20
it.  We believe such strong-mindedness is as rare as it is sound=20
- and absolutely essential to the running of a first-class=20
casualty insurance operation.

     John Seward continues to make solid progress at Home and=20
Automobile Insurance Company, in large part by significantly=20
expanding the marketing scope of that company in general=20
liability lines.  These lines can be dynamite, but the record to=20
date is excellent and, in John McGowan and Paul Springman, we=20
have two cautious liability managers extending our capabilities.

     Our reinsurance division, led by George Young, continues to=20
give us reasonably satisfactory overall results after allowing=20
for investment income, but underwriting performance remains=20
unsatisfactory.  We think the reinsurance business is a very=20
tough business that is likely to get much tougher.  In fact, the=20
influx of capital into the business and the resulting softer=20
price levels for continually increasing exposures may well=20
produce disastrous results for many entrants (of which they may=20
be blissfully unaware until they are in over their heads; much=20
reinsurance business involves an exceptionally =93long tail=94, a=20
characteristic that allows catastrophic current loss experience=20
to fester undetected for many years).  It will be hard for us to=20
be a whole lot smarter than the crowd and thus our reinsurance=20
activity may decline substantially during the projected prolonged=20
period of extraordinary competition.

     The Homestate operation was disappointing in 1979. =20
Excellent results again were turned in by George Billings at=20
Texas United Insurance Company, winner of the annual award for=20
the low loss ratio among Homestate companies, and Floyd Taylor at=20
Kansas Fire and Casualty Company.  But several of the other=20
operations, particularly Cornhusker Casualty Company, our first=20
and largest Homestate operation and historically a winner, had=20
poor underwriting results which were accentuated by data=20
processing, administrative and personnel problems.  We have made=20
some major mistakes in reorganizing our data processing=20
activities, and those mistakes will not be cured immediately or=20
without cost.  However, John Ringwalt has thrown himself into the=20
task of getting things straightened out and we have confidence=20
that he, aided by several strong people who recently have been=20
brought aboard, will succeed.

     Our performance in Worker=92s Compensation was far, far better=20
than we had any right to expect at the beginning of 1979.  We had=20
a very favorable climate in California for the achievement of=20
good results but, beyond this, Milt Thornton at Cypress Insurance=20
Company and Frank DeNardo at National Indemnity=92s California=20
Worker=92s Compensation operation both performed in a simply=20
outstanding manner.  We have admitted - and with good reason -=20
some mistakes on the acquisition front, but the Cypress purchase=20
has turned out to be an absolute gem.  Milt Thornton, like Phil=20
Liesche, follows the policy of sticking with business that he=20
understands and wants, without giving consideration to the impact=20
on volume.  As a result, he has an outstanding book of business=20
and an exceptionally well functioning group of employees.  Frank=20
DeNardo has straightened out the mess he inherited in Los Angeles=20
in a manner far beyond our expectations, producing savings=20
measured in seven figures.  He now can begin to build on a sound=20
base.

     At yearend we entered the specialized area of surety=20
reinsurance under the management of Chet Noble.  At least=20
initially, this operation will be relatively small since our=20
policy will be to seek client companies who appreciate the need=20
for a long term =93partnership=94 relationship with their reinsurers. =20
We are pleased by the quality of the insurers we have attracted,=20
and hope to add several more of the best primary writers as our=20
financial strength and stability become better known in the=20
surety field.

     The conventional wisdom is that insurance underwriting=20
overall will be poor in 1980, but that rates will start to firm=20
in a year or so, leading to a turn in the cycle some time in=20
1981.  We disagree with this view.  Present interest rates=20
encourage the obtaining of business at underwriting loss levels=20
formerly regarded as totally unacceptable.  Managers decry the=20
folly of underwriting at a loss to obtain investment income, but=20
we believe that many will.  Thus we expect that competition will=20
create a new threshold of tolerance for underwriting losses, and=20
that combined ratios will average higher in the future than in=20
the past.

     To some extent, the day of reckoning has been postponed=20
because of marked reduction in the frequency of auto accidents -=20
probably brought on in major part by changes in driving habits=20
induced by higher gas prices.  In our opinion, if the habits=20
hadn=92t changed, auto insurance rates would have been very little=20
higher and underwriting results would have been much worse.  This=20
dosage of serendipity won=92t last indefinitely.

     Our forecast is for an average combined ratio for the=20
industry in the 105 area over the next five years.  While we have=20
a high degree of confidence that certain of our operations will=20
do considerably better than average, it will be a challenge to us=20
to operate below the industry figure.  You can get a lot of=20
surprises in insurance.

     Nevertheless, we believe that insurance can be a very good=20
business.  It tends to magnify, to an unusual degree, human=20
managerial talent - or the lack of it.  We have a number of=20
managers whose talent is both proven and growing. (And, in=20
addition, we have a very large indirect interest in two truly=20
outstanding management groups through our investments in SAFECO=20
and GEICO.) Thus we expect to do well in insurance over a period=20
of years.  However, the business has the potential for really=20
terrible results in a single specific year.  If accident=20
frequency should turn around quickly in the auto field, we, along=20
with others, are likely to experience such a year.

<i>Insurance Investments</i>

     In recent years we have written at length in this section=20
about our insurance equity investments.  In 1979 they continued=20
to perform well, largely because the underlying companies in=20
which we have invested, in practically all cases, turned in=20
outstanding performances.  Retained earnings applicable to our=20
insurance equity investments, not reported in our financial=20
statements, continue to mount annually and, in aggregate, now=20
come to a very substantial number.  We have faith that the=20
managements of these companies will utilize those retained=20
earnings effectively and will translate a dollar retained by them=20
into a dollar or more of subsequent market value for us.  In=20
part, our unrealized gains reflect this process.

