     We currently believe that equity markets in 1980 are likely=20
to evolve in a manner that will result in an underperformance by=20
our portfolio for the first time in recent years.  We very much=20
like the companies in which we have major investments, and plan=20
no changes to try to attune ourselves to the markets of a=20
specific year.

     Since we have covered our philosophy regarding equities=20
extensively in recent annual reports, a more extended discussion=20
of bond investments may be appropriate for this one, particularly=20
in light of what has happened since yearend.  An extraordinary=20
amount of money has been lost by the insurance industry in the=20
bond area - notwithstanding the accounting convention that allows=20
insurance companies to carry their bond investments at amortized=20
cost, regardless of impaired market value.  Actually, that very=20
accounting convention may have contributed in a major way to the=20
losses; had management been forced to recognize market values,=20
its attention might have been focused much earlier on the dangers=20
of a very long-term bond contract.

     Ironically, many insurance companies have decided that a=20
one-year auto policy is inappropriate during a time of inflation,=20
and six-month policies have been brought in as replacements. =20
=93How,=94 say many of the insurance managers, =93can we be expected to=20
look forward twelve months and estimate such imponderables as=20
hospital costs, auto parts prices, etc.?=94 But, having decided=20
that one year is too long a period for which to set a fixed price=20
for insurance in an inflationary world, they then have turned=20
around, taken the proceeds from the sale of that six-month=20
policy, and sold the money at a fixed price for thirty or forty=20
years.

     The very long-term bond contract has been the last major=20
fixed price contract of extended duration still regularly=20
initiated in an inflation-ridden world.  The buyer of money to be=20
used between 1980 and 2020 has been able to obtain a firm price=20
now for each year of its use while the buyer of auto insurance,=20
medical services, newsprint, office space - or just about any=20
other product or service - would be greeted with laughter if he=20
were to request a firm price now to apply through 1985.  For in=20
virtually all other areas of commerce, parties to long-term=20
contracts now either index prices in some manner, or insist on=20
the right to review the situation every year or so.

     A cultural lag has prevailed in the bond area.  The buyers=20
(borrowers) and middlemen (underwriters) of money hardly could be=20
expected to raise the question of whether it all made sense, and=20
the sellers (lenders) slept through an economic and contractual=20
revolution.

     For the last few years our insurance companies have not been=20
a net purchaser of any straight long-term bonds (those without=20
conversion rights or other attributes offering profit=20
possibilities).  There have been some purchases in the straight=20
bond area, of course, but they have been offset by sales or=20
maturities.  Even prior to this period, we never would buy thirty=20
or forty-year bonds; instead we tried to concentrate in the=20
straight bond area on shorter issues with sinking funds and on=20
issues that seemed relatively undervalued because of bond market=20
inefficiencies.

     However, the mild degree of caution that we exercised was an=20
improper response to the world unfolding about us.  You do not=20
adequately protect yourself by being half awake while others are=20
sleeping.  It was a mistake to buy fifteen-year bonds, and yet we=20
did; we made an even more serious mistake in not selling them (at=20
losses, if necessary) when our present views began to=20
crystallize. (Naturally, those views are much clearer and=20
definite in retrospect; it would be fair for you to ask why we=20
weren=92t writing about this subject last year.)

     Of course, we must hold significant amounts of bonds or=20
other fixed dollar obligations in conjunction with our insurance=20
operations.  In the last several years our net fixed dollar=20
commitments have been limited to the purchase of convertible=20
bonds.  We believe that the conversion options obtained, in=20
effect, give that portion of the bond portfolio a far shorter=20
average life than implied by the maturity terms of the issues=20
(i.e., at an appropriate time of our choosing, we can terminate=20
the bond contract by conversion into stock).

     This bond policy has given us significantly lower unrealized=20
losses than those experienced by the great majority of property=20
and casualty insurance companies.  We also have been helped by=20
our strong preference for equities in recent years that has kept=20
our overall bond segment relatively low.  Nevertheless, we are=20
taking our lumps in bonds and feel that, in a sense, our mistakes=20
should be viewed less charitably than the mistakes of those who=20
went about their business unmindful of the developing problems.

     Harking back to our textile experience, we should have=20
realized the futility of trying to be very clever (via sinking=20
funds and other special type issues) in an area where the tide=20
was running heavily against us.

     We have severe doubts as to whether a very long-term fixed-
interest bond, denominated in dollars, remains an appropriate=20
business contract in a world where the value of dollars seems=20
almost certain to shrink by the day.  Those dollars, as well as=20
paper creations of other governments, simply may have too many=20
structural weaknesses to appropriately serve as a unit of long=20
term commercial reference.  If so, really long bonds may turn out=20
to be obsolete instruments and insurers who have bought those=20
maturities of 2010 or 2020 could have major and continuing=20
problems on their hands.  We, likewise, will be unhappy with our=20
fifteen-year bonds and will annually pay a price in terms of=20
earning power that reflects that mistake.

     Some of our convertible bonds appear exceptionally=20
attractive to us, and have the same sort of earnings retention=20
factor (applicable to the stock into which they may be converted)=20
that prevails in our conventional equity portfolio.  We expect to=20
make money in these bonds (we already have, in a few cases) and=20
have hopes that our profits in this area may offset losses in=20
straight bonds.

     And, of course, there is the possibility that our present=20
analysis is much too negative.  The chances for very low rates of=20
inflation are not nil.  Inflation is man-made; perhaps it can be=20
man-mastered.  The threat which alarms us may also alarm=20
legislators and other powerful groups, prompting some appropriate=20
response.

     Furthermore, present interest rates incorporate much higher=20
inflation projections than those of a year or two ago.  Such=20
rates may prove adequate or more than adequate to protect bond=20
buyers.  We even may miss large profits from a major rebound in=20
bond prices.  However, our unwillingness to fix a price now for a=20
pound of See=92s candy or a yard of Berkshire cloth to be delivered=20
in 2010 or 2020 makes us equally unwilling to buy bonds which set=20
a price on money now for use in those years.  Overall, we opt for=20
Polonius (slightly restated): =93Neither a short-term borrower nor=20
a long-term lender be.=94

<i>Banking</i>

     This will be the last year that we can report on the=20
Illinois National Bank and Trust Company as a subsidiary of=20
Berkshire Hathaway.  Therefore, it is particularly pleasant to=20
report that, under Gene Abegg=92s and Pete Jeffrey=92s management,=20
the bank broke all previous records and earned approximately 2.3%=20
on average assets last year, a level again over three times that=20
achieved by the average major bank, and more than double that of=20
banks regarded as outstanding.  The record is simply=20
extraordinary, and the shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway owe a=20
standing ovation to Gene Abegg for the performance this year and=20
every year since our purchase in 1969.

     As you know, the Bank Holding Company Act of 1969 requires=20
that we divest the bank by December 31, 1980.  For some years we=20
have expected to comply by effecting a spin-off during 1980. =20
However, the Federal Reserve Board has taken the firm position=20
that if the bank is spun off, no officer or director of Berkshire=20
Hathaway can be an officer or director of the spun-off bank or=20
bank holding company, even in a case such as ours in which one=20
individual would own over 40% of both companies.

     Under these conditions, we are investigating the possible=20
sale of between 80% and 100% of the stock of the bank.  We will=20
be most choosy about any purchaser, and our selection will not be=20
based solely on price.  The bank and its management have treated=20
us exceptionally well and, if we have to sell, we want to be sure=20
that they are treated equally as well.  A spin-off still is a=20
possibility if a fair price along with a proper purchaser cannot=20
be obtained by early fall.

     However, you should be aware that we do not expect to be=20
able to fully, or even in very large part, replace the earning=20
power represented by the bank from the proceeds of the sale of=20
the bank.  You simply can=92t buy high quality businesses at the=20
sort of price/earnings multiple likely to prevail on our bank=20
sale.

<i>Financial Reporting</i>

     During 1979, NASDAQ trading was initiated in the stock of=20
Berkshire Hathaway This means that the stock now is quoted on the=20
Over-the-Counter page of the Wall Street journal under=20
=93Additional OTC Quotes=94.  Prior to such listing, the Wall Street=20
journal and the Dow-Jones news ticker would not report our=20
earnings, even though such earnings were one hundred or more=20
times the level of some companies whose reports they regularly=20
picked up.

     Now, however, the Dow-Jones news ticker reports our=20
quarterly earnings promptly after we release them and, in=20
addition, both the ticker and the Wall Street journal report our=20
annual earnings.  This solves a dissemination problem that had=20
bothered us.

     In some ways, our shareholder group is a rather unusual one,=20
and this affects our manner of reporting to you.  For example, at=20
the end of each year about 98% of the shares outstanding are held=20
by people who also were shareholders at the beginning of the=20
year.  Therefore, in our annual report we build upon what we have=20
told you in previous years instead of restating a lot of=20
material.  You get more useful information this way, and we don=92t=20
get bored.

     Furthermore, perhaps 90% of our shares are owned by=20
investors for whom Berkshire is their largest security holding,=20
very often far and away the largest.  Many of these owners are=20
willing to spend a significant amount of time with the annual=20
report, and we attempt to provide them with the same information=20
we would find useful if the roles were reversed.

     In contrast, we include no narrative with our quarterly=20
reports.  Our owners and managers both have very long time-
horizons in regard to this business, and it is difficult to say=20
anything new or meaningful each quarter about events of long-term=20
significance.

     But when you do receive a communication from us, it will=20
come from the fellow you are paying to run the business.  Your=20
Chairman has a firm belief that owners are entitled to hear=20
directly from the CEO as to what is going on and how he evaluates=20
the business, currently and prospectively.  You would demand that=20
in a private company; you should expect no less in a public=20
company.  A once-a-year report of stewardship should not be=20
turned over to a staff specialist or public relations consultant=20
who is unlikely to be in a position to talk frankly on a manager-
to-owner basis.

     We feel that you, as owners, are entitled to the same sort=20
of reporting by your manager as we feel is owed to us at=20
Berkshire Hathaway by managers of our business units.  Obviously,=20
the degree of detail must be different, particularly where=20
information would be useful to a business competitor or the like. =20
But the general scope, balance, and level of candor should be=20
similar.  We don=92t expect a public relations document when our=20
operating managers tell us what is going on, and we don=92t feel=20
you should receive such a document.

     In large part, companies obtain the shareholder constituency=20
that they seek and deserve.  If they focus their thinking and=20
communications on short-term results or short-term stock market=20
consequences they will, in large part, attract shareholders who=20
focus on the same factors.  And if they are cynical in their=20
treatment of investors, eventually that cynicism is highly likely=20
to be returned by the investment community.

     Phil Fisher, a respected investor and author, once likened=20
the policies of the corporation in attracting shareholders to=20
those of a restaurant attracting potential customers.  A=20
restaurant could seek a given clientele - patrons of fast foods,=20
elegant dining, Oriental food, etc. - and eventually obtain an=20
appropriate group of devotees.  If the job were expertly done,=20
that clientele, pleased with the service, menu, and price level=20
offered, would return consistently.  But the restaurant could not=20
change its character constantly and end up with a happy and=20
stable clientele.  If the business vacillated between French=20
cuisine and take-out chicken, the result would be a revolving=20
door of confused and dissatisfied customers.

     So it is with corporations and the shareholder constituency=20
they seek.  You can=92t be all things to all men, simultaneously=20
seeking different owners whose primary interests run from high=20
current yield to long-term capital growth to stock market=20
pyrotechnics, etc.

     The reasoning of managements that seek large trading=20
activity in their shares puzzles us.  In effect, such managements=20
are saying that they want a good many of the existing clientele=20
continually to desert them in favor of new ones - because you=20
can=92t add lots of new owners (with new expectations) without=20
losing lots of former owners.

     We much prefer owners who like our service and menu and who=20
return year after year.  It would be hard to find a better group=20
to sit in the Berkshire Hathaway shareholder =93seats=94 than those=20
already occupying them.  So we hope to continue to have a very=20
low turnover among our owners, reflecting a constituency that=20
understands our operation, approves of our policies, and shares=20
our expectations.  And we hope to deliver on those expectations.

<i>Prospects</i>

     Last year we said that we expected operating earnings in=20
dollars to improve but return on equity to decrease.  This turned=20
out to be correct.  Our forecast for 1980 is the same.  If we are=20
wrong, it will be on the downside.  In other words, we are=20
virtually certain that our operating earnings expressed as a=20
percentage of the new equity base of approximately $236 million,=20
valuing securities at cost, will decline from the 18.6% attained=20
in 1979.  There is also a fair chance that operating earnings in=20
aggregate dollars will fall short of 1979; the outcome depends=20
partly upon the date of disposition of the bank, partly upon the=20
degree of slippage in insurance underwriting profitability, and=20
partly upon the severity of earnings problems in the savings and=20
loan industry.

     We continue to feel very good about our insurance equity=20
investments.  Over a period of years, we expect to develop very=20
large and growing amounts of underlying earning power=20
attributable to our fractional ownership of these companies.  In=20
most cases they are splendid businesses, splendidly managed,=20
purchased at highly attractive prices.

     Your company is run on the principle of centralization of=20
financial decisions at the top (the very top, it might be added),=20
and rather extreme delegation of operating authority to a number=20
of key managers at the individual company or business unit level. =20
We could just field a basketball team with our corporate=20
headquarters group (which utilizes only about 1500 square feet of=20
space).

     This approach produces an occasional major mistake that=20
might have been eliminated or minimized through closer operating=20
controls.  But it also eliminates large layers of costs and=20
dramatically speeds decision-making.  Because everyone has a=20
great deal to do, a very great deal gets done.  Most important of=20
all, it enables us to attract and retain some extraordinarily=20
talented individuals - people who simply can=92t be hired in the=20
normal course of events - who find working for Berkshire to be=20
almost identical to running their own show.

     We have placed much trust in them - and their achievements=20
have far exceeded that trust.



                                    Warren E. Buffett, Chairman
March 3, 1980

